        <div id="etch-or-stain" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Etch or Stain? Tell Them Apart First
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before trying to fix a mark on marble, work out which kind of mark it is, because the two need opposite treatments. A stain is something that has soaked into the stone and left color behind: a dark oil shadow, a pinkish wine mark, a brown coffee ring. An etch is a mark with little or no color, usually lighter or whiter than the stone, that looks like a dull water spot or a ghost of the glass that sat there.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look at the mark at a low angle with a light behind it. An etch interrupts the reflection: the polished surface shines and the etched area does not. Run a fingertip across it. Deeper etches often feel very slightly rougher than the polished stone around them. A stain, by contrast, sits under the surface and does not change the shine or the texture at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            Think about what was there. Lemon, lime, vinegar, wine, tomato sauce, soda, fruit juice and many bathroom cleaners are acidic, and all of them etch marble. Oil, coffee, tea, ink and colored foods stain. Some spills, such as red wine, do both, leaving a dull etched ring with a colored stain inside it, in which case you treat the stain first and the etch second.
          </p>

          <p>
            If it is a stain, our guide to <a href="/how-to-remove-stains-from-marble/">removing stains from marble</a> covers the poultice method for each type. Everything that follows here is about etches. Polishing compounds and abrasives do nothing useful for a stain, and a poultice does nothing for an etch, so getting this first step right saves a lot of wasted effort.
          </p>

