        <div id="why" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Why Acid Etches Even Sealed Marble
          </h2>

          <p>
            Marble is made mostly of calcium carbonate, the same mineral as limestone and chalk. Calcium carbonate reacts with acids, which dissolve a thin layer of the stone where they touch it. That is chemistry rather than damage from neglect, and it happens on brand-new, freshly sealed marble just as readily as on an old untreated counter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Impregnating sealers work inside the pores of the stone, lining them so liquids are slower to soak in. They do not put a protective layer over the surface, which means acid still reaches the calcium carbonate and reacts with it. As our <a href="/best-marble-sealer/">marble sealer guide</a> puts it, sealing protects against staining, not etching. That is not a weakness of any particular product; it is how penetrating sealers work.
          </p>

          <p>
            Surface films and protective sheets can block etching, because they physically separate the acid from the stone. Topical coatings, though, tend to scratch and wear unevenly on working countertops, which is why we generally steer people away from them, as explained in <a href="/impregnating-vs-topical-stone-sealer/">impregnating vs topical stone sealer</a>. Professionally applied clear protective films are a separate category that some owners choose for kitchens where etching is a constant problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            Speed and concentration both matter. A drop of lemon juice wiped off within seconds may leave nothing visible, while the same drop left for ten minutes can leave a clear mark. That is the practical lesson: on marble, wiping acidic spills immediately is more important than any product, and keeping acids off the stone in the first place is better still.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="polished" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Light Etches on Polished Marble: Polishing Powder or Paste
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most everyday etches on polished marble are shallow and can be removed at home with a marble polishing powder or etch-remover paste. These products are sold specifically for marble and other calcium-based stone, and they restore the polish on the affected spot through a combination of fine abrasion and, in many formulas, a chemical reaction with the stone surface. Follow the instructions on the specific product, as methods differ.
          </p>

          <p>
            The typical process is simple. Clean and dry the area. Apply a small amount of powder or paste, dampened as directed, and rub it into the etch with a soft cloth or a felt pad, using firm, circular strokes for a minute or two. Wipe it off, rinse with clean water, dry, and inspect it at a low angle. Repeat a few times if the etch is still visible, checking between passes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Test any polishing product on an inconspicuous area first, such as inside the sink cutout or on the back edge. Some polishing compounds are designed only for polished marble and can create a shiny patch on honed stone, and some colored marbles react differently from white ones. A small test shows how your particular stone responds before you work on a visible spot.
          </p>

          <p>
            A low-speed drill or polisher with a felt or soft buffing pad can make the work faster on larger etches, but it also makes it easier to overheat the stone or create an uneven shine. Keep the speed low, keep the pad moving, and stop often to check. Hand polishing is slower but more forgiving, and for a few water-glass rings it is usually enough.
          </p>

          <p>
            Polishing can disturb the sealer in that small area, since it reworks the top of the stone. Once you are happy with the finish, let the spot dry and run a quick water test. If it now darkens faster than the surrounding stone, reseal just that patch following our guide to <a href="/how-to-seal-marble/">sealing marble</a>, feathering the coat into the surrounding area.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="honed" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Etches on Honed Marble
          </h2>

          <p>
            Honed marble has a matte finish, so etches show far less than they do on polished stone. Often the mark is visible only as a slightly lighter or slightly smoother area, and sometimes it is invisible except at a low angle. That is the main reason many people choose honed marble for kitchens: it does not stop etching, but it hides it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not use a polishing compound on honed marble unless the product is explicitly made for honed finishes. Polishing an etch on a matte surface usually creates a small shiny patch, which can be more noticeable than the etch it replaced. The aim on honed marble is to match the surrounding matte texture, not to restore a gloss that was never there.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a visible etch on honed stone, the usual home approach is to blend it with a fine abrasive. Very fine sanding pads or diamond hand pads in the grit range that matches your honed finish, worked lightly and evenly over the area with water, can even out the texture. Work slightly beyond the edge of the etch so there is no line, and check frequently. If you are unsure which grit matches, a stone supplier or fabricator can usually tell you.
          </p>

          <p>
            Many owners of honed marble kitchens simply live with light etching, and over time the surface develops an even, softly worn look sometimes called a patina. If the marks bother you, a periodic professional hone every few years can refresh the whole surface uniformly, which is easier than chasing individual marks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="deep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Deep Etches: Hand Honing, and Where It Stops
          </h2>

          <p>
            Some etches are too deep for a polishing powder. If repeated polishing leaves a visible dull patch, or the mark feels distinctly rough or slightly sunken, the acid has removed more stone than a polish can restore. The fix is to remove a little more stone around the etch so that the surface is level again, then polish it back up through progressively finer abrasives.
          </p>

          <p>
            That process is called honing, and on a small area it can be done by hand with diamond hand pads used wet, starting at a coarser grit to level the etch and working up through finer grits until the finish matches. On polished marble, the last step is usually a polishing powder or the finest grits to bring the shine back. It takes patience, and it is easy to create a visible dip or a patch with a slightly different shine.
          </p>

          <p>
            Know where to stop. If an etch is larger than a palm, sits in the middle of a prominent surface, or is on valuable stone such as a vintage piece or a premium Calacatta slab, the risk of creating a more visible repair goes up sharply. In those cases, practicing on an offcut or calling a professional is more sensible than experimenting on the counter itself.
          </p>

          <p>
            Any honing removes sealer from the treated area, because it removes the top layer of stone. After honing and polishing, clean the area, let it dry completely, and reseal it. If a professional has honed or repolished a whole countertop, treat the entire surface as freshly unsealed and seal it fully, using a product from our <a href="/best-marble-sealer/">best marble sealers</a> list.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pro" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. When to Call a Stone Restoration Professional
          </h2>

          <p>
            A stone restoration specialist hones and polishes marble with wet diamond abrasives on machines designed for the job, and can refinish an entire countertop or floor to a uniform finish. That is the right call when etching is widespread, when DIY polishing has left patchy shine, or when the stone is valuable enough that a mistake would be expensive.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pricing varies by region, the size of the area and the depth of the damage, so get quotes rather than relying on national averages. Ask whether the price includes resealing after the work, what grit they finish to, and whether they can match polished or honed to the rest of the stone. A good restorer will usually test a small area first and show you the result.
          </p>

          <p>
            Professional refinishing is also an opportunity to change the finish. Some owners who are tired of etches on polished marble ask for the countertop to be honed to a matte finish, which hides future etching far better. Others with older, uniformly etched stone ask for a full repolish. Either way, the work removes the existing sealer and should be followed by a thorough reseal.
          </p>

          <p>
            Floors are worth handing to a professional more readily than countertops, because the area is large and uneven DIY work is very visible across a room. A professional floor hone and polish will also remove the general wear and dullness of walkways, not just individual etches.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prevent" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Preventing the Next Etch
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most effective prevention costs nothing: wipe acidic spills immediately. Keep a damp cloth handy when cooking with citrus, wine or tomatoes, and wipe the counter as you go rather than at the end. Etching depends on contact time, so seconds matter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use coasters under glasses, a tray for oils and vinegars, and a cutting board for food preparation. Bottles of olive oil and vinegar left on the counter are a classic source of both stains and etched rings. Bathroom vanities benefit from a tray for perfumes, mouthwash and toiletries, many of which are acidic.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use the right cleaner. Vinegar-based sprays, lemon-scented cleaners, many bathroom descalers and most disinfecting wipes contain acids that etch marble every time they are used. A pH-neutral stone cleaner, as described in our guide to <a href="/how-to-clean-marble-countertops/">cleaning marble countertops</a>, avoids that. Using the wrong cleaner daily causes more widespread dullness than any single spill.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the stone sealed anyway. Sealer does not stop etching, but it does stop the stains that often come along with acidic spills, such as red wine, and it makes cleanup quicker. Testing every few months, as covered in <a href="/how-often-to-seal-marble/">how often to seal marble</a>, keeps that protection in place.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trying to clean an etch away</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Etches are missing stone, not something on the stone, so cleaners, stain removers and poultices cannot remove them. Scrubbing harder with the wrong product often widens the dull area. Identify it as an etch, then polish or hone.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Resealing to fix etching</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealer works inside the pores and does nothing for acid damage on the surface. Adding sealer over an etch can leave residue that makes the spot look worse. Polish the etch first, then reseal only if the water test says the area absorbs.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Polishing a honed finish</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Marble polishing compounds can leave a glossy patch on honed or matte marble, trading a dull etch for a shiny spot. On honed stone, blend etches with fine abrasive pads matched to the finish, or leave light etches alone.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping a test patch</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Polishing powders and abrasive pads behave differently on different marbles and finishes. Test on an inconspicuous spot first and check the result in good light before working on a visible area.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using acidic cleaners every day</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many all-purpose, bathroom and disinfecting products are acidic and etch marble a little with every use. The result is widespread dullness that looks like wear. Switch to a pH-neutral stone cleaner to stop creating new etches.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can etching on marble be removed?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, in most cases. Light etches on polished marble can usually be polished out at home with a marble polishing powder or paste. Deeper etches need honing with fine abrasives and then polishing. Widespread or deep etching is best handled by a stone restoration professional with wet diamond equipment.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does baking soda remove etching from marble?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Baking soda paste is sometimes suggested as a mild abrasive, and it may slightly reduce a very light etch on polished stone, but it is not designed for marble and can leave the area uneven. A purpose-made marble polishing powder is more effective and more predictable. Test either one on a hidden spot first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will sealing marble prevent etching?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Impregnating sealers protect against staining by slowing absorption, but they do not cover the surface, so acids still react with the stone. Only a surface barrier, such as a professionally applied protective film, physically prevents etching. Wiping acidic spills quickly remains the best everyday protection.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my marble have dull water rings?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Rings left by glasses are usually etches from acidic drinks such as wine, juice, soda or cocktails, or from condensation that carried acid from the glass. They are dull and colorless rather than stained. They can be polished out, and coasters prevent them.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is etching on marble permanent?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It does not go away on its own, but it is repairable because it is a surface defect. Polishing restores the shine on light etches and honing levels deeper ones. Many owners of honed marble choose to leave light etching, which softens into a uniform worn look over time.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does professional marble etch repair cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It varies widely with the size of the area, the depth of the etching, the finish and local rates, so it is worth getting several quotes. Ask whether resealing is included, whether they will test a small area first, and whether they can match the existing polished or honed finish.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a drill to polish out etching?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A drill or low-speed polisher with a soft felt pad can speed up polishing on larger etches, but it also increases the risk of overheating the stone or creating an uneven shine. Keep the speed low, keep the pad moving and check often. Hand polishing is slower but more controlled.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

